<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasi Viswanathar Temple, Tenkasi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">begun 1440s</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kasi Viswanathar Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> at Tenkasi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was begun in the 1440s by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arikesari-parakrama-pandya">Arikesari Parakrama Pandya</a>, and the town's name, 'Southern Kashi', recalls the sacred city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, whose temple of Vishwanath it was meant to echo. Its tall gateway tower and sculpted pillared halls dominate the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasi-viswanathar-temple-tenkasi.jpg" alt="Kasi Viswanathar Temple, Tenkasi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pandiaeee · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_magnificent_Kasi_Vishwanathat_Temple_Rajagopuram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legend of its founding, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king Parakrama Pandyan wished to build a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> at Tenkasi and set out for Kasi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, to bring back a lingam. Shiva appeared to him in a dream and told him to follow an army of ants. The next day the king found the ants, understood that he had been kept from going to Varanasi by divine will, and built the temple where the anthill ended. The town of Tenkasi grew up around the temple. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> became independent of the Vijayanagar Empire in 1559 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a>, the temple received gifts from that dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple, in the Tamil style of architecture, is enclosed by concentric granite walls. A pillared hall leads from the entrance to the flagstaff hall, and the east-facing sanctum, on the axis of the entrance, holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as Kasi Viswanathar in the form of a lingam, with images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Vinayaka</a> and Subramanya on either side of the approach. The shrine of his consort Parvathi as Ulagamman stands parallel to it, and smaller shrines of Dakshinamurthy, Arumugar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, the Navagrahas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a> lie in the precinct.</p><p>Composite columns of Virabhadra holding a sword and horn are additions of the kings of Vijayanagar in the early 1500s, of a kind also found at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple">Meenakshi Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, the Nellaiappar Temple in Tirunelveli and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple">Ramanathaswamy Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a>. The temple had a flat tower until 1967, when work began on a seven-tiered gopuram 180 feet high, completed in 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The temple follows the Shaivite tradition, and its priests perform four rituals a day, from Kalasanthi in the morning to the Aravanai Pooja at night, with weekly, fortnightly and monthly rites besides. Its chief festival is Masi Magam in the Tamil month of Masi, February to March; others include Tirukalyanam in Aipassi, Navarathri, Karthikai, Thiruvathirai, Thaipusam, Sivarathri and Panguni Uthiram. It is administered by the Hindu Religious and Endowment Board of the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kasi Viswanathar Temple, Tenkasi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 15th century one branch settled at Tenkasi, where Arikesari Parakrama Pandya began the great <b>Kasi Viswanathar</b> temple in the 1440s.</blockquote><a href="/state/tenkasi-pandyas">Tenkasi Pandyas</a></li></ul></section>
